  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 356 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nOnce the \"cold\" front departs out of the region, surface high \npressure and drier air fills in. This will also mark the start of \nour much needed reprieve from the prolonged heat wave, with \nwidespread afternoon highs in the mid to upper 80s and isolated low \n90s forecast through the early parts of next week. Overnight lows \nwill also be quite comfortable Sunday and Monday night, with \ntemperatures in the 90s expected. In addition, this will also start \na stretch of mostly dry conditions that is expected to last through \nmidweek. When looking broadly at the upper-level pattern, the well \namplified upper ridge over the central CONUS will begin to slowly \nbreak down beginning late Monday and shift eastward towards the \nMidwest and Ohio Valley. This will help shift the lower-evel flow \nback southward and allow warmer air to return, with highs in the low \nto mid 90s forecast Tuesday-Wednesday.\n\nFor the latter portions of the forecast period (late Wednesday and \nbeyond), a more noticeable pattern shift could be in store fore the \nOhio Valley. Guidance is picking up on a robust upper level low \ntrekking towards the NE CONUS and Quebec by late week. As it does \nso, a southward extending cold front will pass through our region, \nproviding the next opportunity for more widespread precipitation. \nThe timing and progression of this system varies greatly across the \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance,resulting in a wide variety of \nsolutions. For example, when looking at the LREF maximum \ntemperatures for Thursday-Saturday the 25-75th percentile spread is \nquite considerable (10-15 degree spread each day), ranging from the \nlow 80s to upper 90s. As a result, our confidence in any particular \noutcome is very low, but will be something to monitor as time \nprogresses.",
